  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "One ray refracts at OA and OB. Preserve alpha, phi, theta, the two normals, and the dashed incident-direction / back-projected emergent-direction construction. Theta depends on phi, incidence alpha, and material index n; no dependence formula or exercise answer is drawn.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": ".intentfold/tickets/STEMROBIN-181/tmp/d435-source193195-first-author/work/10p/pages/0195/figures/fig-160.png",
      "sha256": "e90c7b3abf251e8b960a4093679c256709da05f3015f409b37f0f29e54e1e913"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n74",
        "text": "Optical instruments often use triangular prisms made of glass or other transparent materials.\nThese prisms are among the most important components of instruments used to study the colors of light.\nThey are also used in telescopes, periscopes, and many other devices.\n\nFigure 160 shows a cross-section of a glass prism taken perpendicular to its lateral edges.\nA ray passing through the prism is refracted twice, once at interface $OA$ and once at interface $OB$.\nThe angle $\\varphi$ between the two interfaces is called the **refracting angle of the prism**.\nThe ray's angle of deviation $\\theta$ depends on the prism's refracting angle $\\varphi$, the refractive index $n$ of its material, and the angle of incidence $\\alpha$.\n\nFigure 160\n\nOptical instruments also often use glass prisms whose bases are right isosceles triangles.\nThe arrangements discussed here use ordinary glass prisms in air, for which the critical angle for total internal reflection from glass into air is less than $45^\\circ$.\nUnder this condition, total internal reflection occurs at the relevant reflecting surfaces in the arrangements shown in Figures 161, 162, and 163.\nIn Figure 161, the prism turns the beam through $90^\\circ$, as required in a periscope.\nPrism binoculars use the light path shown in Figure 162, which changes the beam's direction by $180^\\circ$.\nFigure 163 shows how a prism reverses the vertical order of rays emerging from an optical instrument: the upper ray becomes the lower ray, and the lower ray becomes the upper ray.\n\nFigure 161\n\nFigure 162\n\nFigure 163"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n74",
        "text": "光学仪器中常用由玻璃或其他透明材料制成的三棱镜。\n这类棱镜是研究光的颜色的仪器中最重要的部件之一。\n望远镜、潜望镜和许多其他装置也会用到三棱镜。\n\n图 160 展示玻璃三棱镜的一个截面，截面垂直于侧棱。\n光线通过棱镜时，在交界面 $OA$ 和 $OB$ 上各折射一次。\n两个交界面之间的角 $\\varphi$ 叫做**棱镜的折射角**。\n光线的偏向角 $\\theta$ 取决于棱镜的折射角 $\\varphi$、棱镜材料的折射率 $n$ 和入射角 $\\alpha$。\n\n图 160\n\n光学仪器中也常用底面为等腰直角三角形的玻璃棱镜。\n这里讨论的是普通玻璃棱镜置于空气中的装置，玻璃到空气的全反射临界角小于 $45^\\circ$。\n在这一条件下，图 161、162、163 所示的相应反射处都发生全反射。\n图 161 中的棱镜使光束偏转 $90^\\circ$，潜望镜需要这种光路。\n棱镜双筒望远镜利用图 162 所示的光路，使光束的方向改变 $180^\\circ$。\n图 163 展示利用棱镜改变从光学仪器射出的光线的上下次序：上方的光线变到下方，下方的光线变到上方。\n\n图 161\n\n图 162\n\n图 163"
      }
    ],
